Close every remaining AP->CP content gap against the capture

Sequence-aligning r47 against stock on identical firmware left 60 of stock's
196 requests on ch 0xf5 missing or out of order. About forty of those are the
same ids at different moments -- MM's polling cadence, which appears in both
the missing and the extra list. The rest were genuine content gaps, and this
closes all of them.

**SET_INDICATION_FILTER sent the wrong byte.** Stock sends `ff 00 00 00`; we
sent `7f 00 00 00`. Six named bits plus bit 7, which nothing we have names.
This was previously asserted to be byte-identical on the strength of a dump of
our own side only -- it was never compared against stock's.

**GET_RADIO_CAPABILITY, 1 -> 4 per stack.** The request carries no payload at
all, so the three extra occurrences go in the boot-parity table at stock's own
positions. Tables are now 124 and 122 rows.

**GET_FACILITY_LOCK, one 0x10/0x03 pair per application.** Stock issues four
requests on the stack whose SIM carries an ISIM and two on the one that does
not, the second pair differing only in the AID:

    ch0xf6  facility 0x10  AID a0 00 00 00 87 10 02 ff 33 ...   (USIM)
    ch0xf6  facility 0x03  AID a0 00 00 00 87 10 02 ff 33 ...
    ch0xf6  facility 0x10  AID a0 00 00 00 87 10 04 ff ff ...   (ISIM)
    ch0xf6  facility 0x03  AID a0 00 00 00 87 10 04 ff ff ...

The client cached only apps[0], so the ISIM pass was impossible. It now keeps
every AID the card reports and the pass walks them.

**The UICC access-control probe, which we sent none of** -- seven 0x0247, three
0x020f and one 0x020e on stack 0. It is carrier-privilege discovery: look for
the GSMA access-rule applets by AID, and when they are absent read EF_DIR to
enumerate the card's applications.

    0x0247 open by AID A0000001514143 4C ("..QACL", ARA-M) -> ffffffff 6a82
    0x0247 open by AID A000000063 "PKCS-15" (ARF)          -> ffffffff 6a82
    0x0247 open by AID A000000018 "GPAC-15"                -> ffffffff 6a82
    0x0247 open with NO AID                                -> channel 1, 9000
    0x020f SELECT 3F00, SELECT 2F00, READ RECORD 1
    0x020e close the channel

This could never have been a table row, which is why it went unclosed for so
long: the channel number comes back in the open response and every later frame
carries it, so a verbatim replay would transmit on a channel the CP never gave
us and leak the one it did. Hence an explicit sequence that always closes what
it opened, including on error. All reads; nothing is written to the card and
nothing is reported -- it exists for parity.

**GET_SIM_SLOT_STATUS, 3 -> 1.** Stock reads the inventory once per boot; we
read it three times inside 40 ms, once in mm_sit_cp_prepare() and once per
modem from load_sim_slots(). prepare() polls until both logical stacks are
mapped, so it holds the authoritative answer by the time either modem gets
that far; the modems now read that instead of asking again, and the cache is
dropped on SIM_SLOT_STATUS_CHANGED so a hot-swap still re-reads.

The boot-config fake peer gains the eight UICC frames and asserts the channel
reaches every one of them and that the close happens. Writing that fixture
caught a mistake in it rather than in the code: with the status word byte-
swapped (`00 90` instead of the capture's `90 00`) the client correctly took
the no-channel path, and the suite hung on the frames it then never sent.

